				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ashton-hennessy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6459445); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6459445&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608972724" alt="Ashton Hennessy " style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6459445)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ashton-hennessy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6459445); return false;" >Ashton Hennessy </a></span> <span class="odate time_1590347054 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 May 2020 19:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4661291">
									

<p>When writing an SCP concept to put up for review, I struggle a lot to keep under the 400 word limit. I find it difficult to squeeze the concept, the narrative, explanations of possible plotholes in the narrative, and an explanation of what makes this narrative <em>interesting</em>, in 400 words.</p>
<p>I'm not complaining (well, I am a bit!), since obviously brevity is obviously useful for a writer to help them streamline their concepts, and it's convenient for reviewers. But it's hard.</p>
<p>So, um, any tips on keeping SCP concept pitches brief?</p>

							</div>

								
