			<div class="content" id="post-content-4661783">
									

<p>I took a quick look at the thread. Since the narrative section is the largest, here's how I would trim it down to focus on major story beats and less on the fluff, as well as be more thrifty with language. Also, I moved some material out since it wasn't story material so much as description of the entity itself.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Show&nbsp;Original</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Special Containment Procedures are to not alter or copy this document, and to surround it with procedurally generated narratives, backed-up with *huge number* computing power to be used by *fancy name* AI upon a containment breach.</p>
<p>Standard pomo-SCP fare, y'know? Should tip you off that this is a format-screw: which it is, with the SCP angrily introducing itself before engaging a clinical tone-y explanation of what it is — interjecting with angry reminders that 'the Foundation made me write this'.</p>
<p>- Progressed from bargain bin to local newspaper before capture.</p>
<p>- It's got a unique ideology: believes it's morally wrong to write a work of fiction where a character suffers (partially influenced by the fact that it itself suffers when inside such a narrative). To stop this, it turns sad narratives into happy ones.</p>
<p>- Being sad through a fictional character is immoral, because the construct in your mind is suffering — like a pantheistic God who lets people suffer: He's still being mean, even though those people are Himself.</p>
<p>- The implications of its ideology: essentially, all negative ideas are bad, and, in a transhumanist-y-type way, the entire mental landscape of humanity should be changed to be composed of only happiness.</p>
<p>- Was difficult for the Foundation to write about it, because any information about it always had an extremely short conceptual distance from the narrative it was currently residing in, since it's a part of own narrative. It always mucked up anything they tried to write about it!</p>
<p>- As 'punishment', the Foundation dumped it into 'eternal suffering’ narratives until it stopped 'vandalizing' stuff written about it.</p>
<p>- The skip's 'fight' against these narratives fried up the AI and wasted resources, so the article's current state is acceptable enough for both parties that they aren't willing to act for now.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<hr />
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Show&nbsp;trimmed</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>ConProcs state the document cannot be altered or copied, and must be surrounded with procedurally generated narratives. If a breach occurs, an AI assigned to this is backed-up with *huge number* computing power. This hints that the page is a format screw. The SCP will angrily introduce itself and constantly interrupt its clinical documentation with angry reminders that 'the Foundation made me write this'.</p>
<p>The Foundation had trouble writing about the entity due to its ability to affect its own documentation. As 'punishment', the Foundation dumped it into 'eternal suffering’ narratives until it stopped 'vandalizing' stuff written about it. Eventually the entity's struggling fried up the AI and wasted resources, so the article's current state is acceptable enough for both parties that they aren't willing to act for now.</p>
<p>&#8212;-</p>
<p>The entity moved from a bargain bin to local newspaper before capture. Partially due to personal bias, it believes it's morally wrong to write fiction involving suffering characters. Being sad through a fictional character is also immoral, because the construct in your mind is suffering. To stop this, it turns sad narratives into happy ones.</p>
<p>Its ideology implies all negative ideas are bad, and, in a transhumanist-y-type way, the entire mental landscape of humanity should be changed to be solely happiness.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>302 words to 210 words. I think the thing to keep in mind here is to not get too caught up in explaining how things work. Naturally, if a reviewer asks for clarification by all means provide those details. To start out though, there's no need to go into a ton of detail.</p>
<p>(I will say though&#8230; this seems like a combination of Fred from Series I and <a href="/scp-000">SCP-000</a>. Maybe take a look at other pages tagged with &quot;meta&quot; to see what's been done before and make sure what you have is distinct?)</p>

							</div>

								
