			<div class="content" id="post-content-4663142">
									

<blockquote>
<p>But I think it has a enough subtle how-it-works differences to these two articles — and largely different, I'd say, narrative differences</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Existing SCP but with differences in personal beliefs&quot; isn't likely to wow the audience.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The core idea, anyway, is the SCP's strange worldview and actions in furthering that worldview</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe try writing this as a tale or Group of Interest format document rather than an SCP article. Given that an SCP article that covers the inner monologue of the entity would rely on a lot of spoonfed narrative anyway (for an entity like this, most of its inner thoughts would be super apparent upon empirical observation alone without it outright saying a lot of what it thinks/is thinking), a tale or GoI format might be a more organic, believable presentation of the same information.</p>

							</div>

								
